  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in Kentucky through FY2026 to date shows a concentrated spend profile. Total obligations are $6.9B, distributed across 71,024 awards with an average award size of $97.4K. Obligations fell 33.7% year over year. The Department of Defense accounts for the large majority of current-year buying at $6.3B, while the next biggest buyers range from $161M to $10.9M. The single largest vendor is HUMANA GOVERNMENT BUSINESS INC at $4.6B, followed by a mix of defense, manufacturing, and service firms. Industry concentration is heavy in Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $4.6B, with several other NAICS categories between $491M and $51.2M. Year-to-date totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity only, not a full fiscal year. These figures show where federal dollars are flowing in the state right now and which agencies and vendors dominate the current contracting landscape.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How large is federal contracting activity in Kentucky right now?",
        "body": "Total obligations in Kentucky are $6.9B for FY2026 to date. Those obligations are split across 71,024 awards with an average award size of $97.4K. Obligations fell 33.7% year over year, reflecting lower current-year totals compared with prior full fiscal years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries are driving that spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the primary buyer at $6.3B. Other buying agencies range from $161M at the Department of the Treasury to $10.9M at the Department of Labor. Industry activity is concentrated in Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $4.6B, with All Other Support Services at $491M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $366M also notable."
      }
    ]
  },
